  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "A certain company produces two types of footwear: casual and sports. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between both types of footwear each year:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Casual** | **Sports** |\n| To: | **Casual** | $0.55$ | $0.7$ |\n| **Sports** | $0.45$ | $0.3$ |  |\n\nThat is, $45%$ of casual customers switch to sports each year, and $70%$ of sports customers change to casual.\n\nCurrently, $700\\,000$ customers purchase casual footwear, while $300\\,000$ customers purchase sports footwear. How many customers will purchase sports footwear in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change.",
  "worked_solution": "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.55 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.45 & 0.3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of footwear is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}700\\,000 \\\\ 300\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}700\\,000 \\\\ 300\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.55 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.45 & 0.3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.595 \\\\ 0.405\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one year, $40.5%$ of customers will purchase sports footwear. Computing $40.5%$ of $1\\,000\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\,000\\cdot 0.405=405\\,000.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $405\\,000$ customers will purchase sports footwear in one year.",
